6. Canonical Identity Findings

Packet, logical-intent, idempotency, authorization-candidate, transition,
disposition, invalidation, attempt, projection, request, decision, receipt,
and history identities are domain-separated and rebuilt from exact canonical
materials. Exact-type validators reject malformed values, bool-as-int,
custom-equality substitutes, reordered material, and coherent identity drift.
Root selection binds the packet authorization candidate rather than treating
logical intent as authority.

7. Lifecycle and Transition Findings

The 26-rule profile is exact. CREATED is Root-bound and non-executable.
Activation acquires reservation atomically. Queue, pending, block, expiry,
revocation, supersession, fulfillment, receipt, non-consuming failure, retry,
and uncertain terminal transitions are rule-bound and history-preserving.
Unknown transitions fail closed. t04, t24, and t26 are mutually exclusive
outcomes of one invocation.

8. Idempotency and Immutable History Findings

UNCLAIMED, RESERVED, CONSUMED, and UNCERTAIN_CLOSED are distinct. Six exact
event classes reconstruct immutable 16-field disposition history. There is no
release event. t24 appends no disposition event and preserves exact RESERVED
owner and history. CONSUMED and UNCERTAIN_CLOSED are permanent.

9. Root-Bound Invalidation, Expiry and Supersession Findings

Dependency acceptance, revocation, and supersession use separate
non-self-referential candidates and accepted post-Root bindings. Mandatory
dependency freshness, expiry, accepted kill switches, revocation, and
supersession deterministically remove executability without allowing
Registry-created authority.

10. Corridor, Effect Firewall and Receipt Findings

Corridor recomputes current eligibility and ownership before fulfillment.
EffectRequest fields are exactly projected from packet, Root, permission,
scope, time, and idempotency contracts. Runtime capability state is private
to one invocation. Receipt is exact immutable EvidenceReceipt material and
creates no authority, permission, future permission, FinalOutput, or effect
handle.

11. Replay and Present-Inspection Findings

Historical validation projects immutable Firewall authorization without
constructing EffectFirewallV01, EffectCapabilityV01, or mutable invocation
state. Replay uses recorded time and cannot mutate or execute. Present
inspection uses injected current time and cannot rewrite history. Legacy and
A4B t24 histories are replayed under separate exact evidence laws. NO_RETRY
never reports retry eligibility.

12. Two-Domain Invariance Findings

The deterministic AIRLINE and SUPPLIER fixtures use distinct packet,
idempotency, Root-decision, invalidation, and history identities while sharing
one packet family, Transition Registry, and Root authority law. Both become
BLOCKED with RESERVED ownership preserved. Cross-domain substitution fails
closed. Closed domain programs are not rerun.

13. Living Gauntlet and Conformance Findings

Living Gauntlet v1.1 executes 14 active acts with 14 PASS, including one real
bounded action_packet_lifecycle act. Kernel Conformance v0.2 reports 11/11
categories, 2/2 domains, and 20/20 negative probes PASS. The lifecycle
category and ten G2-A negative probes are derived from actual validated
runtime observations. Authority, permission, provider, network, Gemini, and
real-effect counters remain zero.

